# {{APP_MODULE}} frontend

Generated from the OlegShevtsov1/1_mvp_sdlc project templates at {{SDLC_VERSION}}.

React + Vite + TypeScript frontend of the {{APP_MODULE}} project; the backend is
[`{{REPO_BE}}`](https://github.com/{{REPO_BE}}). One Docker image (nginx serving the built
bundle) runs in every environment; the backend URL, the error-reporting DSN and the environment
name are read when the container starts (`/config.js`), never baked into the bundle.

The project name and DNS zone are written only in [`config/project.env`](config/project.env)
(`PROJECT_NAME={{PROJECT_NAME}}`, `PROJECT_ZONE={{PROJECT_ZONE}}`); the Kamal configs, deploy
scripts and tests derive everything else from it. Rules for working here are in
[`CLAUDE.md`](CLAUDE.md) and `.claude/rules/`.

## Requirements

- Node.js (the version in `.nvmrc`; `nvm use` picks it up) and npm
- Ruby (any recent version; `npm test` renders the Kamal config with it)
- For deploys from a console: Docker, Ruby with `gem install kamal -v 2.12.0`, the AWS CLI,
  access to the shared CI VPN; for prod also
  [crane](https://github.com/google/go-containerregistry/tree/main/cmd/crane) and `jq`

## Local setup

```bash
nvm use
npm ci
git config core.hooksPath scripts/git-hooks   # pre-push hook: published v* tags are immutable
npm run dev                                   # dev server on http://localhost:3000
```

Without a runtime config the page shows `API status: not configured` and error reporting stays
off. To talk to a local backend, create the gitignored `public/config.js`:

```js
window.APP_CONFIG = {
  API_URL: "http://localhost:3001",
  SENTRY_DSN: "",
  ENV_NAME: "local",
  APP_VERSION: "local",
};
```

## Commands

The checks Front CI runs (`.github/workflows/front-ci.yml`), in its order; the list is kept in
sync with `CLAUDE.md` §Commands:

```bash
gitleaks git --redact --exit-code 1 --verbose .   # secrets in the full history
bash scripts/check-public-repo.sh                 # no internal hosts, IPs, IDs or local paths
actionlint -color                                 # workflow lint
npm run lint                                      # ESLint
npm run typecheck                                 # tsc -b
npm test                                          # Vitest: src/ and tests/
npm audit --audit-level=high
npm run build                                     # production bundle in dist/
bash scripts/check-bundle-config.sh src dist      # no environment values in src/ or dist/
bash scripts/check-asset-hashes.sh dist           # bundle file names carry a content hash
```

`npm run e2e` runs the Playwright checks in `e2e/` against a deployed app (not part of CI), e.g.
`E2E_BASE_URL=https://app.dev.{{PROJECT_ZONE}} E2E_ENV_NAME=dev npm run e2e`.

## GitHub secrets and variables

Names only (taken from the workflows); values are never committed. `PROD_VPN_IP` is set by
`project-add`; the others must exist before the workflow that reads them runs.

Repository secrets:

- `AWS_ROLE_ARN` — the role the workflows assume for ECR (OIDC)
- `VPN_CI_OVPN` — the shared CI VPN profile
- `SRV_DEPLOY_SSH_KEY` — SSH key of the dev host's deploy user
- `{{AI_REVIEW_SECRET}}` — AI PR review (only used when `AI_REVIEW` is `on`)
- `RAG_MCP_URL`, `RAG_MCP_TOKEN` — address and token of the shared project knowledge server `mvp-rag` that the
  `mvp-sdlc` plugin connects to (decision 61 b); set by `project-add` (S5), read by no workflow yet

Environment `dev` secrets:

- `SENTRY_DSN` — the dev frontend DSN
- `TELEGRAM_DEV_ALERTS_BOT_TOKEN`, `TELEGRAM_DEV_ALERTS_CHAT_ID`, `TELEGRAM_DEV_ALERTS_THREAD_ID` —
  the project's dev alerts topic

Environment `prod` secrets:

- `PROD_VPN_IP` — the prod host's VPN address (set by `project-add`; never a file)
- `PROD_SSH_KEY` — SSH key of the prod host
- `SENTRY_DSN` — the prod frontend DSN
- `TELEGRAM_PROD_ALERTS_BOT_TOKEN`, `TELEGRAM_PROD_ALERTS_CHAT_ID`, `TELEGRAM_PROD_ALERTS_THREAD_ID` —
  the project's prod alerts topic
- `BE_REPO_READ_TOKEN` — fine-grained token, Contents: read on the backend repository (release-order
  check)
- `GLITCHTIP_API_URL`, `GLITCHTIP_API_TOKEN` — release-watch reads the prod frontend project's
  issues

Repository variables:

- `RUNS_ON` — runner label for every job (default `ubuntu-latest`)
- `DEV_AUTO_DEPLOY` — `true` deploys dev after every green Front CI push to `develop` or `hotfix/*`
- `AI_REVIEW` — `on` enables the AI PR review; anything else keeps it off

The workflows use the shared composite actions `vpn-up`, `vpn-down` and `notify-telegram` from
`OlegShevtsov1/1_mvp_infra@env/dev`.

## Branch model

Work branches off `develop`: `feature/<KEY>-<n>-<slug>` or, for a fix of production,
`hotfix/<KEY>-<n>-<slug>`, where `<KEY>` is `PROJECT_NAME` from `config/project.env` in capitals,
`<n>` the card number and `<slug>` comes from the card title. The Archon factory's
`archon/task-factory-*` branches are accepted too. Features reach `develop` by squash pull request;
hotfixes go to `main`, then back to `develop`; the operator merges `develop → main` (the release PR)
and hotfix PRs by hand. Every PR title is `<KEY>-<n>: <card title>` with exactly one
`Type: feat|fix|chore|docs|refactor|test|ci` line in the body (`pr-conventions-check`). Details:
[`CLAUDE.md`](CLAUDE.md), `.claude/rules/git.md`.

Checks on pull requests: Front CI, `tdd-check` (the first commit of a code change is a failing
`test(...)`), `pr-conventions-check`, `changelog-check` and, when enabled, `ai-review`.
`main-guard` and `develop-guard` check that every commit on `main`/`develop` came from a merged PR.

## Board flow

The project's Trello board has nine lists, in this order: `Backlog`, `Ready`, `In Progress`, `Review`, `Test`,
`Blocked`, `Ready to Release`, `Test after Release`, `Done`. The operator moves a card to `Ready` once the
Definition of Ready is met; from there the harness moves it (the factory to `In Progress`, `Review` and `Test`;
post-merge and the release runs onward); `Blocked` holds a card that waits for the operator or something
external. A renamed list goes under `trello.lists` in `.archon/factory-profile.yaml`. Who moves the card at each
step: [Board flow](https://github.com/OlegShevtsov1/1_mvp_sdlc/blob/{{SDLC_VERSION}}/docs/sdlc/03-dor-dod.md#board-flow).

## Deploy

- Dev: Dev Deploy ([`.github/workflows/dev-deploy.yml`](.github/workflows/dev-deploy.yml)) builds
  the image once, pushes it to ECR and deploys it with Kamal to the shared dev host as
  `app.dev.{{PROJECT_ZONE}}` (`config/deploy.yml` + `config/deploy.dev.yml`). Runs after a green
  Front CI push on `develop` or `hotfix/*` when `DEV_AUTO_DEPLOY` is `true`, or on demand:
  `gh workflow run dev-deploy.yml --ref develop`.
- Prod: Kamal Deploy ([`.github/workflows/kamal-deploy.yml`](.github/workflows/kamal-deploy.yml))
  promotes the dev image unchanged (no rebuild) and deploys a release tag to the prod host as
  `app.{{PROJECT_ZONE}}`, behind the `kamal-proxy` the backend already runs there
  (`config/deploy.prod.yml`):
  `gh workflow run "Kamal Deploy" --ref main -f environment=prod -f version=v<X.Y.Z> -f confirm=prod`.
  Kamal Manage ([`.github/workflows/kamal-manage.yml`](.github/workflows/kamal-manage.yml)) runs
  `status`, `logs`, `restart`, `rollback` and `remove`.
- Console equivalents: `deploy/deploy.sh`, `deploy/rollback.sh`, `deploy/promote.sh`,
  `deploy/manage.sh` (they print variable names, never values).

### Stopping and starting by hand

Kamal names containers after the version it runs: `sha-<git sha>` on dev, `prod-sha-<git sha>` on
prod. Prefer `deploy/manage.sh restart`. When you must start by hand, read the running version with
`kamal app version -d <dev|prod> -q` and always pass it:

```bash
kamal app start -d prod --version prod-sha-<full git sha>
kamal app start -d dev --version sha-<full git sha>
```

### Monitoring (Beszel)

The frontend has no Beszel accessory. It shares the prod host with the backend (it joins the
backend's `kamal-proxy`), and a host runs one Beszel agent: the agent is the backend's Kamal
accessory (see the backend README). A second agent would collide on its port and report the same
host twice. The shared dev host's agent is deployed by the infrastructure repository.

## Releases and hotfixes

Prod runs only released versions: annotated tags `vX.Y.Z` on `main`, each with a section in
[`CHANGELOG.md`](CHANGELOG.md). In short:

1. A `Type: feat` or `Type: fix` PR adds its entry under `## [Unreleased]` (or carries the label
   `skip-changelog`); every section keeps `Minimum backend version: vX.Y.Z`, equal to
   [`deploy/min-be-version`](deploy/min-be-version).
2. Release prep PR into `develop` renames `[Unreleased]` to the number
   `scripts/next-version.sh origin/develop` prints; then the release PR `develop → main`, titled
   `<KEY>-<n>: Release vX.Y.Z`, with `## Changelog` and the version's CHANGELOG section in its body. Both PRs carry
   the release's own card, never `<KEY>-0` (`pr-conventions-check` fails it).
3. Tag the merge commit: `git tag -a vX.Y.Z -m vX.Y.Z origin/main && git push origin vX.Y.Z`.
   The Release workflow checks the tag and creates a draft GitHub Release from the CHANGELOG
   section; the operator publishes it.
4. Deploy the tag with Kamal Deploy. The backend release named in `deploy/min-be-version` must be
   on prod first (`deploy/be_version_check.sh`); `release-watch` then watches prod for 10 minutes.

A hotfix branch `hotfix/<KEY>-<n>-<slug>` from `main` goes by PR into `main` with its own
`## [X.Y.Z]` section, is tagged and deployed the same way, then back-merged into `develop` with a
merge commit. Published tags are never moved or deleted.

